{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CfukNsENJ8nFd3qDR8XZC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, but not with a plain Cloudflare Worker. Workers handle HTTP(S) and WebSocket traffic, so they can't bind to port 5432 or act as a general TCP listener. Cloudflare's **TCP sockets API** (`cloudflare:sockets`, `connect()`) lets a Worker make outbound raw TCP connections, which is how a Worker can talk to a Postgres server, but it can't accept inbound TCP. For a Postgres proxy that listens on 5432, you'd need to run it on a server or container (for example, a VM or Cloudflare Containers) and use a Worker only as an HTTP or WebSocket front end."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3041,"cache_read_input_tokens":535,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3041},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d52e6423-aa88-4fbc-9f26-42d24e53f491","uuid":"1d32ae1b-eb19-486e-a471-28c820db5bde","timestamp":"2026-10-11T03:35:11.116Z","request_id":"req_011CfukNs3U4k7mH5EjZd7sb"}
